# Colour (CO)

This is the short form. Before writing code in this area, read the full rules with reasons, bad/good examples and checks: `.ui-polish/rules/12-colour.md`.

- **CO-01 Build the palette from the brand colour** (error). Take the primary colour from `design.md`. Derive what you need from it in OKLCH: a hover/pressed step (lightness ±0.04 to 0.06), a tinted surface (`color-mix(in oklch, var(--primary) 10%, var(--background))`), and the `--ring`. Verify `--primary-foreground` on `--primary` is at least 4.5:1; if the brand colour is too light for white text, use dark text on it rather than changing the brand colour. If no brand colour exists, propose two or three options suited to the audience (not indigo or violet by default) and let the owner choose.
- **CO-02 A tinted neutral scale does most of the work** (warning). Neutrals carry 80 to 90% of the interface: page, cards, borders, text. Use one scale with a slight tint toward the brand hue (chroma about 0.003 to 0.02 in OKLCH), never pure `#000`, `#fff`-on-`#000`, or a hue-less grey next to a warm brand. Map roles to steps: background (lightest), muted/secondary surface, border, input border (3:1), muted text (4.5:1 at least), body text (not pure black: about L 0.2). Do not mix two neutral families (slate with stone).
- **CO-03 Semantic colours have fixed meanings and full pairs** (error). Define `--destructive` (red: errors, deletion), `--success` (green: completed, positive), `--warning` (amber: needs attention, reversible risk) and `--info` (blue or neutral: neutral notices), each with a `-foreground` that passes 4.5:1 and a way to make a soft surface (10 to 15% mix). Use each only for its meaning: red is never decoration, green never just "primary". Tune their lightness and chroma to sit with the brand rather than taking pure `#ff0000` / `#00ff00`. If the brand colour is red or green, pick semantic hues that stay distinguishable from it.
- **CO-04 The accent is rare** (warning). The primary colour marks the one main action per view, the current selection, links and focus. As a rough proportion: 60% background neutral, 30% secondary surfaces and text, 10% or less brand colour. One filled primary button per section; other actions are outline, ghost or link. Do not colour large backgrounds, every icon, every heading or every card border with the brand colour.
- **CO-05 Data colours are a designed set** (warning). Charts and categorical labels use a dedicated set of chart tokens (`--chart-1` … `--chart-6`) chosen to be distinguishable from each other in both themes and for colour-blind viewers (vary lightness as well as hue; add direct labels or patterns). Use a single-hue ramp for sequential data and a two-hue diverging ramp for above/below data. Do not reuse the semantic red/green for arbitrary series, and do not use the library's default rainbow.
- **CO-06 Check every pair, in both themes** (error). After defining or changing tokens, measure contrast for each text-on-surface pair that the interface uses: foreground/background, card-foreground/card, muted-foreground on background, muted and card, primary-foreground/primary, each semantic foreground/its colour, and link colour on background. All must reach 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text and for `--input`/`--ring` against the surface). Record the measured ratios in `design.md`. Never place text on an image without a scrim or a solid plate.
